Nitro for linux-riscv-security-tool
===================================

This tree builds a dynamically loadable RISC-V `nitro.ko` for the common IEE
security-tool interface.

Architecture
------------

Nitro uses the raw syscall tracepoints and the reserved Nitro security-tool ID:

    RISC-V raw_syscalls tracepoint
        -> nitro.ko: TGID/TID and syscall filtering
        -> nitro.ko: pt_regs extraction and no-fault pathname capture
        -> iee_security_tool_invoke(IEE_SECURITY_TOOL_NITRO_NG, ...)
        -> nitro.ko IEE callback: fixed-size event ring operation

At load time the module claims its ID with `iee_security_tool_register()`.
Publish, pop, statistics, and clear operations all enter through that callback.
The kernel requires `CONFIG_IEE_SECURITY_TOOLS=y`; no Nitro-specific in-tree
gate opcode is required.

The 256-record ring is module-owned memory so Nitro remains dynamically
loadable. Ring mutations execute through IEE, but the module's outer mapping is
not read-only like a built-in `.iee.data` backend would be.

Build for RISC-V
----------------

Build the target kernel first, then run:

    make KDIR=../linux-riscv-security-tool ARCH=riscv \
      CROSS_COMPILE=/opt/spacemit-toolchain-linux-glibc-x86_64-v1.2.4/bin/riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu-

The module imports the common IEE and tracepoint symbols. The Makefile uses
`Module.symvers` when present and falls back to `vmlinux.symvers` for an
Image-only kernel build.

Load
----

Capture selected common RISC-V syscalls for one process group:

    insmod nitro.ko target_tgid=1234 syscalls=56,63,64,94,221 capture_paths=1

Capture every process and optionally log records:

    insmod nitro.ko target_tgid=-1 log_events=1

Capture is disabled by default (`target_tgid=0`). `target_pid` can narrow the
selection to one thread; an empty syscall array selects every syscall.

Interface
---------

`/dev/nitro` is a root-only binary event stream. Batched `read(2)`, `poll(2)`,
`NITRO_IOC_GET_STATS`, and `NITRO_IOC_CLEAR` are supported by the ABI in
`nitro.h`. If the ring is full, the oldest event is replaced and `dropped` is
incremented.

Security boundary
-----------------

Ring operations execute inside IEE, while ring storage, tracepoint
registration, filtering, pathname capture, the device, and module lifecycle
remain owned by the dynamically loaded module. This validates isolated dispatch
and centralizes ring mutation, but does not make module data read-only to the
outer kernel. Strong retained-record protection still requires a built-in
protected storage backend.
